Give the Tinker Board's SDIO wifi time to settle after reset

The RTL8723DS on mmc1 is marginal at init. On Armbian's 6.18 kernel it fails
SDIO enumeration twice and only succeeds on the third attempt, once the MMC core
has stepped the init clock down 400kHz -> 300kHz -> 200kHz:

    [2.961] mmc_host mmc1: Bus speed = 400000Hz ... mmc1: error -110
    [3.031] mmc_host mmc1: Bus speed = 300000Hz ... mmc1: error -110
    [3.099] mmc_host mmc1: Bus speed = 200000Hz
    [3.145] mmc1: new high speed SDIO card at address 0001

On mainline 7.2.6 with PREEMPT_RT the identical ladder runs but every rung fails,
including a fourth at 187.5kHz, and the card never appears:

    [2.477] 400000Hz -> error -110
    [2.519] 300000Hz -> error -110
    [2.563] 200000Hz -> error -110      <- this one works on 6.18
    [2.616] 187500Hz -> error -110
    [2.659] mmc1: Failed to initialize a non-removable card

The difference is timing, not configuration: the device trees are byte-identical
for both mmc@ff0d0000 and sdio-pwrseq, and the whole wifi/MMC Kconfig is
identical too. The failing sequence is simply faster -- 2.477-2.659s against
6.18's 2.947-3.145s -- and "allocated mmc-pwrseq" to the first attempt is 13ms.

mmc-pwrseq-simple defaults post-power-on-delay-ms to 0, so the chip is driven the
instant its reset GPIO is released. 6.18 got away with it; a kernel that reaches
the same code sooner does not. Ask for a real settle delay rather than relying on
the rest of the kernel being slow.

20ms is chosen to be comfortably more than the RTL8723x power-on requirement
while adding nothing measurable to boot time.

--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3288-tinker.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3288-tinker.dtsi
@@ -66,6 +66,7 @@
 		clock-names = "ext_clock";
 		pinctrl-names = "default";
 		pinctrl-0 = <&wifi_enable>;
+		post-power-on-delay-ms = <20>;
 		reset-gpios = <&gpio4 RK_PD3 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>,
 			<&gpio4 RK_PD4 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 	};
